# knowledge-ctx individuation output — per-session, regenerated, never source.
# Each of these names itself AUTO-GENERATED in its own header. They reached a PR
# once (#142) because a coder worktree had individuated a profile and a broad
# `git add` swept 3,000 lines of another product's skills into a doc-comment
# commit. Ignoring them makes that impossible rather than caught in review.
#
# Naming those three files individually left the rest of `.claude/` unignored,
# and on this machine nothing showed it: the operator's personal global ignore
# (`~/.config/git/ignore`) carries `**/.claude/settings.local.json`, so
# `git status` reads clean here while a fresh clone or the self-hosted runner
# would stage that file on a plain `git add -A`. Check the way the steward did,
# with the global config out of the picture:
#
#     git -c core.excludesFile=/dev/null check-ignore -v .claude/settings.local.json
#
# So everything under `.claude/` is ignored. This repo used to track five files
# there (the three deck-* agents and the handoff-leave/pickup skills), named
# back in with negations. They were hand-placed copies of KB documents
# (`projects/deck/agents/`, `projects/platform/skills/`) and had fallen behind
# them, and once `co -r` individuates the deck/daytime profile into the primary
# checkout, tracked copies would leave it dirty on every rotation. The KB is
# the source; the Individuator writes `agents/`, `skills/`, `CLAUDE.md`,
# `.active-profile`, `.materialized` and `.profile-documents.json` here, and
# this one rule covers all of them.
.claude/*
